---
import StandardPageLayout from "../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro";
import FinancialStatementKey from "../../components/FinancialStatementKey.astro";
import Glossary from "../../components/Glossary.astro";
import PageToc from "../../components/PageToc.astro";
import "../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../styles/extended.css";
import HistoryTimeline from "../../components/HistoryTimeline.astro";
import history from "../../data/history.json";
import metadata from "../../data/standards/usgaap/asc974.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.description}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
  bannerNotice={metadata.bannerNotice}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "Conceptual Framework Linkage — a Tax Status, Not a Distinct GAAP Model" },
        { id: "s2", label: "Scope" },
        { id: "s3", label: "Organizational and Offering Costs" },
        { id: "s4", label: "Property Acquisition Costs" },
        { id: "s5", label: "Funds From Operations (FFO) — a Non-GAAP Supplemental Metric" },
        { id: "s6", label: "Worked Example — Offering Cost Treatment" },
        { id: "s7", label: "Worked Example — an FFO Reconciliation From Net Income" },
        { id: "s8", label: "Disclosure Requirements" },
        { id: "s9", label: "Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates" },
        { id: "s10", label: "No Dedicated IFRS REIT Standard — Discussion" },
        { id: "s11", label: "Statement Mapping" },
        { id: "s12", label: "Related Topics" },
        { id: "s13", label: "Glossary" }
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>
  <div class="wrap">

    <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage — a Tax Status, Not a Distinct GAAP Model</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">REIT status is defined by tax law, not accounting standards</div><div style="font-size: var(--text-small); color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.6;">"REIT" status is FUNDAMENTALLY a TAX concept — governed by Internal Revenue Code Subchapter M requirements (e.g. a 75% real estate ASSET test, a 90% taxable income DISTRIBUTION requirement) — rather than a distinct GAAP MEASUREMENT model. A REIT's underlying real estate investments continue to be accounted for under GENERAL real estate principles (ASC 970, ASC 360), with ASC 974 addressing only a FEW genuinely REIT-specific accounting matters: organisational/offering costs and property acquisition costs.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Entities electing REIT tax status</div><div style="font-size: var(--text-small); color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.6;">ASC 974 applies to entities that HAVE elected (or intend to elect) REIT tax status, addressing the LIMITED set of accounting matters genuinely distinctive to this structure.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>Organizational and Offering Costs</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Netted against offering proceeds, similar to general stock issuance costs</div><div style="font-size: var(--text-small); color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.6;">Costs of a REIT's SECURITIES OFFERING (underwriting fees, legal, printing costs directly related to the offering) are treated CONSISTENTLY with general stock issuance cost principles applicable to ANY entity — CHARGED AGAINST the GROSS PROCEEDS of the offering (i.e. NETTED against additional paid-in capital), rather than expensed through the income statement. This is NOT a genuinely unique REIT rule — it simply confirms that REITs follow the SAME general offering cost treatment as other equity-issuing entities.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Property Acquisition Costs</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Governed by general ASC 805/360 principles</div><div style="font-size: var(--text-small); color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.6;">Costs of ACQUIRING real estate properties are accounted for under GENERAL business combination (ASC 805, if the acquisition constitutes a business) or asset acquisition principles — with TRANSACTION costs generally EXPENSED as incurred for business combinations, or CAPITALISED as part of the asset's cost for asset acquisitions that don't meet the definition of a business — the SAME distinction and treatment ANY entity would apply, not a REIT-specific rule.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Funds From Operations (FFO) — a Non-GAAP Supplemental Metric</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">A widely used industry metric, defined by NAREIT — NOT itself a GAAP-mandated measure</div><div style="font-size: var(--text-small); color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.6;">"FUNDS FROM OPERATIONS" (FFO) — GENERALLY defined (by the National Association of Real Estate Investment Trusts, NAREIT) as GAAP net income, EXCLUDING gains/losses from property SALES and ADDING BACK real estate DEPRECIATION/AMORTISATION — is a WIDELY used supplemental performance metric in REIT industry practice and investor communications, reflecting the view that GAAP depreciation may not fully capture real estate's actual economic value trends (since well-maintained real estate often APPRECIATES rather than declining in value the way GAAP depreciation assumes). <strong>FFO is NOT itself a GAAP measure MANDATED by ASC 974</strong> — it is a widely adopted, INDUSTRY-STANDARD non-GAAP supplemental disclosure, typically presented ALONGSIDE (and reconciled to) GAAP net income, not as a substitute for it.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Worked Example — Offering Cost Treatment</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Netted against proceeds, not expensed</div>
    <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: A REIT's public offering</div><div class="wk-body">A REIT raises $200,000,000 gross proceeds in a public equity offering, incurring $9,000,000 of underwriting fees and other direct offering costs.</div><div class="wk-answer">The REIT records $200,000,000 − $9,000,000 = <strong>$191,000,000</strong> as the NET increase to additional paid-in capital — the $9,000,000 of offering costs are NETTED directly against the gross proceeds, NOT recognised as an expense in the income statement.</div></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Worked Example — an FFO Reconciliation From Net Income</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">A common supplemental disclosure, reconciled to GAAP net income</div>
    <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: Reconciling GAAP net income to FFO</div><div class="wk-body">A REIT reports GAAP net income of $40,000,000, which includes a $12,000,000 gain on the sale of a property and $28,000,000 of real estate depreciation and amortisation expense for the period.</div><div class="wk-answer">FFO = $40,000,000 (net income) − $12,000,000 (property sale gain, excluded) + $28,000,000 (depreciation/amortisation, added back) = <strong>$56,000,000</strong> — presented as a SUPPLEMENTAL, NON-GAAP metric ALONGSIDE the $40,000,000 GAAP net income figure, with a clear reconciliation between the two.</div></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">What ASC 974 requires</div><div class="check-list"><span>Treatment of organisational and offering costs</span><span>If FFO or similar supplemental non-GAAP metrics are presented, a clear reconciliation to the most comparable GAAP measure</span></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cn span2"><div class="ctitle cn">Where judgement matters most</div>
    <div class="two-col"><div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Whether a property acquisition constitutes a business combination or an asset acquisition, driving transaction cost treatment</span></div></div>
    <div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Fair value allocations in property acquisitions accounted for as business combinations</span></div></div></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>No Dedicated IFRS REIT Standard — Discussion</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">REIT status is a legal/tax concept without a precise accounting-standard equivalent</div><div style="font-size: var(--text-small); color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.6;">IFRS has NO dedicated REIT standard — much like ASC 974 itself is a fairly THIN Topic, since "REIT" is fundamentally a TAX/LEGAL status rather than a distinct accounting model. Entities operating similarly under IFRS apply IAS 40 (Investment Property) — which offers its OWN notable POLICY CHOICE between the COST model and the FAIR VALUE model for investment property, a genuinely different, more consequential measurement choice than anything specific to REITs under US GAAP (where investment property remains at historical cost less depreciation under ASC 360, WITHOUT an equivalent fair value model option). Many countries have their OWN REIT-like tax regimes, but these are NATIONAL tax law constructs, not IFRS accounting requirements.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Where every ASC 974 event lands</div>
    <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Event</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
    <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Offering costs</span><span><span class="badge be">SE</span></span><span>Netted against gross offering proceeds within paid-in capital</span></div>
    <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>FFO disclosure</span><span>Supplemental, non-GAAP</span><span>Reconciled to GAAP net income, not itself a GAAP measure</span></div>
    </div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Related Topics</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
    <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Topic</span><span>Connection</span></div>
    <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASC 970</span><span>Real Estate—General — supplies the underlying real estate cost capitalisation framework REITs also apply</span></div>
    <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASC 805</span><span>Governs property acquisitions constituting a business combination</span></div>
    <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASC 360</span><span>Governs ongoing depreciation of REIT-held real estate assets</span></div>
    </div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cn span2">
    <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Funds From Operations (FFO)</div><div class="glossary-def">A widely used, NAREIT-defined non-GAAP supplemental metric adjusting GAAP net income for property sale gains/losses and real estate depreciation/amortisation.</div></div>
    <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">REIT (Real Estate Investment Trust)</div><div class="glossary-def">A tax status under Internal Revenue Code Subchapter M requiring specific asset and income distribution tests, rather than a distinct GAAP accounting model.</div></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <FinancialStatementKey />

  </div>
  <section id="history">
    <HistoryTimeline items={history.asc974 || []} />
  </section>
</StandardPageLayout>

